    "title": "Mutual Recursion Sequence",
    "slug": "mutual-recursion-sequence",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "description": "Mutual recursion occurs when two or more functions are defined in terms of each other. A famous example of mutual recursion is the Hofstadter Female and Male sequences ($F$ and $M$), introduced by Douglas Hofstadter in his Pulitzer Prize-winning 1979 book *G\u00f6del, Escher, Bach: an Eternal Golden Braid*.\n\nThe sequences are defined for all non-negative integers as follows:\n- $F(0) = 1$\n- $M(0) = 0$\n- $F(n) = n - M(F(n - 1)), \\quad \\text{for } n > 0$\n- $M(n) = n - F(M(n - 1)), \\quad \\text{for } n > 0$\n\nNotice that the value of $F(n)$ requires evaluating $M$ on the result of $F(n-1)$, and vice versa. Naive evaluation of these mutually recursive functions results in exponential time complexity due to redundant overlapping subproblems.\n\nImplement mutual recursion with memoization in C to compute $F(N)$ and $M(N)$ for $T$ input queries.",
    "input_format": "Line 1: An integer $T$ ($1 \\le T \\le 50$), the number of queries.\nNext $T$ lines: Each line contains a single integer $N$ ($0 \\le N \\le 10^4$).",
    "output_format": "For each query, print: `F(<N>) = <val>, M(<N>) = <val>`.",
    "constraints": "$1 \\le T \\le 50$, $0 \\le N \\le 10^4$.",
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": "4\n0\n1\n5\n10",
        "output": "F(0) = 1, M(0) = 0\nF(1) = 1, M(1) = 0\nF(5) = 3, M(5) = 3\nF(10) = 6, M(10) = 6",
        "explanation": "F(0)=1, M(0)=0. F(1) = 1 - M(F(0)) = 1 - M(1) = 1 - (1 - F(M(0))) = 1 - (1 - F(0)) = 1 - (1 - 1) = 1."
      },
      {
        "input": "2\n2\n4",
        "output": "F(2) = 2, M(2) = 1\nF(4) = 3, M(4) = 2",
        "explanation": "Evaluating mutual recurrence for N=2 and N=4."
      }
    ],
    "execution_mode": "stdio",
    "starter_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint female(int n);\nint male(int n);\n\nint main(void) {\n    // Implement Hofstadter mutual recursion with memoization\n    return 0;\n}\n",
    "hints": [
      "Declare forward function prototypes for both female() and male() functions.",
      "Use memoization arrays initialized to -1 to cache previously computed results.",
      "Base cases: female(0) = 1, male(0) = 0."
    ],
    "explanation": "Mutual recursion requires forward declarations in C so that female() can call male() and male() can call female(). Without memoization, evaluating F(N) branches into multiple nested calls, causing exponential runtime. With memoization arrays memo_F and memo_M, each state from 0 to N is evaluated at most once, reducing the complexity to O(N).",
    "time_complexity": "O(N)",
    "space_complexity": "O(N) for memoization and call stack",
    "common_mistakes": [
      "Omitting forward function declarations, which causes C compiler compilation errors.",
      "Evaluating recursively without memoization, leading to stack overflow or timeout for N > 30.",
      "Mixing up the base cases: F(0) is 1, but M(0) is 0."
    ],
