    "title": "Three Sum to Zero",
    "slug": "three-sum-to-zero",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "description": "Given an integer array $nums$ of $N$ integers, find all unique triplets $[a, b, c]$ such that $a + b + c = 0$ and the triplet elements are distinct elements from the array. Each output triplet must be sorted such that $a \\le b \\le c$. The triplets themselves must be printed in lexicographical order, with one triplet per line. If no triplet sums to zero, print `NONE`. \n\nYour solution must avoid duplicate triplets in the output. For example, given `[-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4]`, the two unique triplets that sum to zero are `[-1, -1, 2]` and `[-1, 0, 1]`. An optimal solution sorts the array first in $O(N \\log N)$ time, then iterates through each element and uses a two-pointer technique to find pairs in $O(N)$ time per element, achieving $O(N^2)$ overall time complexity.",
