    "description": "A phrase is a palindrome if, after converting all uppercase letters into lowercase letters and removing all non-alphanumeric characters, it reads the exact same forward and backward. Alphanumeric characters include lowercase letters, uppercase letters, and ASCII digits. Given a single line of text, determine whether it is a palindrome according to this definition. \n\nYour program must output `true` if the cleaned string is a palindrome, and `false` otherwise. Implement your solution using a two-pointer approach that evaluates characters from both ends directly without copying or allocating a new filtered string, thereby maintaining $O(N)$ time complexity and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. Empty strings or strings containing only non-alphanumeric characters are considered valid palindromes because they read the same in both directions.",
    "input_format": "A single line of text containing alphanumeric characters, spaces, and punctuation symbols (length up to $10^5$).",
    "output_format": "Print `true` if the string is a valid palindrome, or `false` otherwise.",
    "constraints": "1 <= string length <= 10^5\nCharacters are printable ASCII",
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama\n",
        "output": "true\n",
        "explanation": "Stripping punctuation and lowercasing gives 'amanaplanacanalpanama', which is a palindrome."
      },
      {
        "input": "race a car\n",
        "output": "false\n",
        "explanation": "Cleaned string is 'raceacar', which is not symmetric ('raceacar' != 'racaecar')."
      }
    ],
    "execution_mode": "stdio",
    "starter_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\n\nbool is_palindrome(const char *str) {\n    // TODO: Check if string is palindrome ignoring non-alphanumeric and case\n    return false;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    static char buffer[200005];\n    if (!fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), stdin)) return 0;\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", is_palindrome(buffer) ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n    return 0;\n}\n",
    "hints": [
      "Use `isalnum((unsigned char)c)` from `<ctype.h>` to determine if a character is valid.",
      "Use `tolower((unsigned char)c)` to compare corresponding valid characters case-insensitively.",
      "Advance `left` whenever `str[left]` is non-alphanumeric, and decrement `right` whenever `str[right]` is non-alphanumeric."
    ],
    "explanation": "We maintain two pointers `left` at the start and `right` at the end of the input buffer. At each iteration, we skip non-alphanumeric characters until both pointers land on valid characters. We convert both characters to lowercase and check for equality. If they differ, the phrase cannot be a palindrome. If all matching character pairs succeed until pointers cross, the phrase is a palindrome.",
    "time_complexity": "O(n)",
    "space_complexity": "O(1)",
    "common_mistakes": [
      "Not skipping non-alphanumeric characters like spaces, commas, colons, and hyphens.",
      "Failing to convert both characters to lowercase before comparison, causing case mismatches.",
      "Allocating a secondary buffer of size 10^5 when the problem can be solved in-place in O(1) space."
    ],
