    "difficulty": "hard",
    "description": "Implement a manual version of the standard C library function `strstr` without including or calling `strstr`. Given two strings, `haystack` and `needle`, find the 0-based index of the first occurrence of `needle` in `haystack`. If `needle` is not part of `haystack`, your function must return `-1`. If `needle` is an empty string, the return value should be `0`, conforming to standard C library specification. \n\nYour implementation must perform manual character-by-character comparison using pointers. Take care to handle edge cases properly: when the length of `needle` exceeds the length of `haystack`, the function should immediately return `-1` without entering out-of-bounds comparisons.",
    "input_format": "Two lines: the first line contains `haystack` and the second line contains `needle` (lengths up to $10^5$).",
    "output_format": "Print the 0-based index of the first occurrence, or `-1` if needle is not found.",
