    "description": "Sorting an array of strings in C can be computationally expensive if strings are copied repeatedly. An efficient, idiomatic approach creates an array of pointers (`char *lines[N]`) where each pointer references a string buffer in memory. Sorting is then performed by swapping only the pointer addresses rather than copying string characters. \n\nGiven $N$ strings, store each string in memory and initialize an array of pointers pointing to each string. Sort the pointer array in lexicographical ascending order using standard library `qsort` along with a custom comparator function pointer: `int compare_strings(const void *a, const void *b)` Inside the comparator, cast the void pointers properly to pointer-to-pointer-to-const-char: `const char *str_a = *(const char **)a;` and compare with `strcmp`. Print the sorted strings, one per line.",
