    "description": "### Problem Statement\nIn a **multilevel linked list**, in addition to standard `next` pointers, each node may possess a `child` pointer that points to the head of a completely separate sublist. These sublists may in turn contain nodes with their own child sublists, forming a tree-like hierarchy of linked nodes.\n\nDefine the multilevel node:\n```c\ntypedef struct MNode {\n    int val;\n    struct MNode *next;\n    struct MNode *child;\n} MNode;\n```\n\n#### Flattening Rule:\nFlatten the multilevel list into a single standard singly linked list such that:\n- Whenever a node has a `child`, the entire child list is spliced in immediately after the current node in a **depth-first (pre-order)** fashion, followed by the remaining `next` nodes.\n- All `child` pointers in the flattened list must be reset to `NULL`.\n\n#### Input Format:\nTo serialize this multilevel structure simply and cleanly:\n- Line 1: $N$ (number of operations).\n- Operations:\n  - `APPEND <val>`: Append node `<val>` to the tail of the current main level list.\n  - `CHILD <parent_val> <child_val>`: Find the first node with value `<parent_val>`. Attach a new node `<child_val>` as its child (or append to the end of its existing child list).\n  - `FLATTEN`: Flatten the entire hierarchy depth-first and print: `val1 -> val2 -> ... -> NULL` (or `EMPTY` if empty).",
