    "difficulty": "medium",
    "description": "### Problem Statement\nA **stack** is a linear data structure that adheres to the **LIFO (Last In, First Out)** discipline: the most recently inserted element is always the first one to be removed. One of the simplest and most cache-efficient implementations of a stack uses a fixed-capacity contiguous array managed by an integer `top` index.\n\nIn this problem, you will implement a fixed-capacity integer stack in C:\n- The stack holds up to $C$ integers ($C$ is given as input).\n- An integer variable `top` tracks the index of the top element, initialized to `-1` (indicating an empty stack).\n\n#### Supported Operations:\n1. `PUSH <val>`:\n   - If the stack is full (`top == capacity - 1`), print `OVERFLOW`.\n   - Otherwise, increment `top`, store `<val>` at `data[top]`, and do not print anything.\n2. `POP`:\n   - If the stack is empty (`top == -1`), print `UNDERFLOW`.\n   - Otherwise, print `data[top]` and decrement `top`.\n3. `PEEK`:\n   - If the stack is empty (`top == -1`), print `EMPTY`.\n   - Otherwise, print `data[top]` without removing it.\n4. `ISEMPTY`:\n   - Print `TRUE` if `top == -1`, otherwise `FALSE`.\n5. `SIZE`:\n   - Print the current count of elements (`top + 1`).\n\nDeallocate the array buffer before program termination.",
