    "description": "You are given $N$ activities with their respective start times and finish times. A single resource (e.g. a conference room or a single person) can only perform one activity at a time. Two activities $i$ and $j$ are compatible if they do not overlap, meaning the start time of one is greater than or equal to the finish time of the other ($start_j \\ge finish_i$).\n\nFind the maximum number of mutually compatible activities that can be selected.\n\nThe Activity Selection Problem is a classic problem demonstrating the greedy choice property. The optimal greedy strategy is:\n1. Sort all activities in non-decreasing order of their finish times.\n2. Select the first activity (which finishes earliest, leaving the maximum amount of remaining time for future activities).\n3. For each subsequent activity in sorted order, if its start time is greater than or equal to the finish time of the previously chosen activity, select it and update the last finish time.\n\nBecause greedy choice always leaves the maximal remaining time window open, it provably yields an optimal subset of non-overlapping intervals in $O(N \\log N)$ sorting time.",
