    "description": "Given an $M \\times N$ grid filled with non-negative integers, find a path from the top-left cell $(0, 0)$ to the bottom-right cell $(M-1, N-1)$ that minimizes the sum of all numbers along its path.\n\nYou may only move either down or right at any point in time. Because each step must either move right or down, the total length of any valid path is fixed at $(M - 1) + (N - 1)$ steps.\n\nTo find the minimum sum path efficiently:\n1. Let $DP[i][j]$ represent the minimum cost to reach cell $(i, j)$ from $(0, 0)$.\n2. The starting cell cost is simply $DP[0][0] = grid[0][0]$.\n3. For the first row, cells can only be reached from the left: $DP[0][j] = DP[0][j - 1] + grid[0][j]$.\n4. For the first column, cells can only be reached from above: $DP[i][0] = DP[i - 1][0] + grid[i][0]$.\n5. For all other cells $(i, j)$, $DP[i][j] = grid[i][j] + \\min(DP[i - 1][j], DP[i][j - 1])$.\n\nOptimize the space complexity to $O(N)$ by using a 1D DP row, updating values in-place as each row of the grid is processed. Output the minimum path sum as a 64-bit integer.",
    "input_format": "The first line contains two integers: M (rows) and N (columns), where 1 <= M, N <= 500. The next M lines each contain N space-separated non-negative integers representing the grid cells (0 <= grid[i][j] <= 1000).",
    "output_format": "Print a single 64-bit integer representing the minimum path sum from (0,0) to (M-1,N-1).",
    "constraints": "1 <= M, N <= 500, 0 <= grid[i][j] <= 1000",
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": "3 3\n1 3 1\n1 5 1\n4 2 1\n",
        "output": "7\n",
        "explanation": "Path 1 -> 3 -> 1 -> 1 -> 1 minimizes the sum to 7."
      },
      {
        "input": "2 3\n1 2 3\n4 5 6\n",
        "output": "12\n",
        "explanation": "Path 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 6 gives the minimum sum of 12."
      }
    ],
    "execution_mode": "stdio",
    "starter_code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int m, n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &m, &n) != 2) return 0;\n    \n    // Implement grid dynamic programming for minimum path sum\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n",
    "hints": [
      "You can only arrive at cell (i, j) from above (i-1, j) or from left (i, j-1).",
      "Base cases are the first row (only moves from left) and first column (only moves from above).",
      "Compress DP state to a single row array of size N to achieve O(N) space."
    ],
    "explanation": "We can compress the M x N table into a 1D array of size N. As we iterate through row i, dp[j] represents the cost from above before being updated, and dp[j-1] represents the cost from the left. Taking the minimum of the two and adding the current cell cost computes the optimal path.",
    "time_complexity": "O(M * N)",
    "space_complexity": "O(N)",
    "common_mistakes": [
      "Allocating a large 500x500 2D array on the stack which may risk stack overflow in limited environments.",
      "Forgetting to handle the first row and first column where moves are restricted to one direction only.",
      "Using 32-bit int instead of long long when grid cells accumulate."
    ],
